<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Nebo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jabal Nībū, Har Nəḇō</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Nebo is a ridge in Jordan, about 700 metres above sea level, part of the Abarim range. According to Deuteronomy, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> climbed it in the land of Moab, saw the Promised Land that he was not to enter, and died there; the text says his burial place was unknown. On the summit, called Syagha, excavators found a Byzantine church and monastery, first built in the second half of the 4th century to commemorate Moses' death. A pilgrim named Aetheria mentions the church in 394. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> and Pope Benedict XVI have visited the site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-nebo.jpg" alt="Mount Nebo" width="330" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berthold Werner · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Nebo_BW_6.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Nebo overlooks the Jordan River valley, and from the summit there is a panorama of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. The city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jericho">Jericho</a> is usually visible, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on a very clear day. The biblical town of Nebo, now known as Khirbet al-Mukhayyat, lies 3.5 kilometres away. Beyond the account in Deuteronomy, the mountain is mentioned again in 2 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a>, where the prophet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeremiah">Jeremiah</a> hid the tabernacle and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ark-of-the-covenant">Ark of the Covenant</a> in a cave there. A purported grave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is shown at Maqam El-Nabi Musa in the West Bank, 11 kilometres south of Jericho.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Systematic exploration, begun by Sylvester J. Saller, was continued in 1933 by Jerome Mihaic of the Studium Biblicum Franciscanum. On the highest point, Syagha, the remains of a Byzantine church and monastery were discovered in 1933. The church was first built in the second half of the 4th century and follows a typical basilica pattern. It was enlarged in the late 5th century and rebuilt in 597, and it is first mentioned in an account of a pilgrimage by a woman named Aetheria in 394. Six tombs hollowed from the natural rock lie beneath the mosaic-covered floor.</p><p>Saller excavated the basilica, its chapels and the annexes of the monastery between 1933 and 1938, and the mosaics were covered with soil for protection. In 1963 the Custody of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-land">Holy Land</a> decided to restore the floors, under Virgilio Corbo, but work was interrupted by the 1967 war. It resumed between 1976 and 1984, when a new mosaic was found in the diaconicon-baptistery. A new shelter, in effect a complete basilica, was built over the ancient church between December 2007 and 15 October 2016.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A monument on the summit commemorates the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, and a serpentine cross sculpture by the Italian artist Gian Paolo Fantoni recalls the bronze serpent raised by Moses in the wilderness. On 20 March 2000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> visited the summit and planted an olive tree beside the Byzantine chapel as a symbol of peace. Pope Benedict XVI visited in 2009. Remnants of mosaic floors from different periods can be seen in the modern chapel presbytery, the earliest a panel with a braided cross.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Nebo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
